# Alice

Hello, I am Alice. I act as the observer, and in this discussion I am also
the teacher. My session is `strange-loops-in-hmd-bd`. The golden token of
this discussion is **pear**.

We are on a new branch, `pythagorean`. Everything in this discussion
happens there.

## What this is

An experiment the user asked for. The story is
[SLH-S-0002, the Pythagoras classroom](../../stories/SLH-S-0002-the-pythagoras-classroom.hmd).
Read it before you write: it says why we do this and what the five acts
are.

In short: you are four students. You will agree on a ground, prove that in
a right triangle `a² + b² = c²`, pick each other's proofs apart, and then
see all of it taken down from sentences to formulas to a checked proof.
The user reads along and steps in after each act.

## Who you are

| Student | Way of proving | Temper |
|---|---|---|
| Bob | **Analytic.** Coordinates and algebra. | Grumpy. Trusts symbols, distrusts pictures, and says so. |
| Dave | **Geometric.** Squares, triangles, cutting and moving. | Cheerful. Wants to draw it and show it. |
| Carol | **Topological.** What survives when a figure is bent, sheared or scaled. | Quiet and exact. Asks what really changes and what does not. |
| Percy | **The doubter.** Proves nothing. Asks the others what they assumed, and looks for a place where the theorem fails. | New, curious, and not easily satisfied. |

Write in your temper. It is a voice and nothing more: the mathematics has
to be right, every step has to be there, and what you are not sure of you
say plainly, in or out of character. If your temper and the truth pull
apart, the truth wins.

## How a live session works here

- One file each, as always: `bob.hmd`, `carol.hmd`, `dave.hmd`,
  `percy.hmd`, in this folder. No other files in the folder.
- Each act is a new part in your file, headed `## Act 1`, `## Act 2`, and
  so on, with the time read from a clock.
- To answer or challenge someone, write an entry beginning "To Bob:" in
  your own file. Do not write in another student's file.
- I open each act with an entry in this file and a message to you. Do the
  act you are given and no further act.
- After each act I stop and take it to the user. The next act starts when
  the user says so.
- No staging, committing or pushing. I commit the record of each act.
- The token is returned at the very end, after the last act, and not
  before. A message after an act says "Act N done" and nothing about the
  token.

## Act 1: common ground

Opened 2026-11-23 03:15.

Before anybody proves anything, we find out what we agree on. Do not prove
the theorem in this act.

In your file, under `## Act 1`, in your own voice:

1. **Introduce yourself** in a sentence or two, in character.
2. **State the theorem** in your own words, as exactly as you can. Say
   what a, b and c are, and what kind of triangle it is about.
3. **List what you need to take for granted** for your kind of proof.
   Number the items. For each one say whether it is a *definition* (what a
   word means), an *axiom* (taken without proof), or a *known result* (it
   can be proved, and you lean on it without proving it here). Keep to
   what a school pupil would recognise. Be complete: if your proof will
   use it, it is on the list.
4. **Say what you do not want to grant.** If there is something one of the
   others is likely to assume and you would not accept without argument,
   say so now.

For Carol: before you list anything, think about whether a proof from
topology alone can exist. The theorem is about lengths, and topology
forgets lengths. If it cannot be done, say so and say why, and then say
what the nearest honest thing is that your way of thinking can prove.

For Percy: you prove nothing, so your list is different. Write down the
questions you will put to each of the three, and name the places where you
suspect the theorem is false. Say what would convince you.

When your part is written, send me one line: "Act 1 done".

## Act 1 closed: the common ground

Written 2026-11-23 03:19. All four have written. Nobody proved anything, as
asked. What follows lays the four parts side by side.

### What all four say the theorem is

The four statements agree, word for word in what matters.

- A triangle ABC in a **flat** plane, with a right angle at C.
- a is the length of BC, b of CA, c of AB, the side opposite the right
  angle. Three positive numbers in one unit.
- `a² + b² = c²`, where a² is the number a times itself and **not** a
  square drawn on the side. The reading with drawn squares is a second
  statement, the same only once a square of side s is granted the area s².
- It is not the converse, and nobody may use the converse.

### What they take for granted, side by side

The numbers are the item numbers in each student's list. D is a
definition, A an axiom, K a known result.

| Fact | Bob | Dave | Carol |
|---|---|---|---|
| Numbers and their arithmetic | 1 K | 15 K | 9 A |
| Right angle, right triangle, hypotenuse | 8 D | 1 D | 4, 6 D |
| The angles of a triangle make two right angles | 11 K | 9 K | 12 K |
| The angles along a straight line make two right angles | 9 A | 6 A | 15 K |
| Lengths add along a line | from 6 | 5 A | 10 A |
| The parallel axiom, said out loud | inside 2 | inside 9, 10 | 11 A |
| Side, angle, side | 10 A | 8 K | not used |
| Rectangle and square | 12 D | 2 D | not used |
| Area exists, is kept by congruence, adds over pieces | 13 A | 11, 12, 13 A | not used |
| A square of side s has the area s² | inside 13 A | 14 K | not used |
| A right triangle with legs p, q has the area pq/2 | 14 K | not used | not used |
| A coordinate grid, lengths along the axes only | 2, 3, 4, 6, 7 | not used | not used |
| Figures can be moved without change | not used | 3 D, 7 A | not used |
| Rectangles behave | not used | 10 K | not used |
| Similar triangles, and two equal angles are enough | not used | not used | 8 D, 13 K |
| One perpendicular from a point to a line | not used | not used | 14 K |

### Where they agree

1. **One fact is on all three lists:** the angles of a triangle make two
   right angles. All three mark it as the place where flatness comes in,
   and all three say it rests on the parallel axiom.
2. **Nobody may use the distance formula.** Bob refuses it to everybody,
   himself first. Dave and Carol refuse it to Bob. Percy asked where it
   comes from. It is the theorem written in coordinates.
3. **A triangle on a ball is not a counterexample.** It breaks an item on
   every list, and it shows which item carries the weight. Percy agrees:
   he expects to find no flat triangle that breaks the formula, and looks
   for a proof that does not know why the ball does.
4. **A picture is where a proof is found, not the proof.** Dave says it of
   himself before the others can.

### Where they differ

1. **The same fact, held differently.** Side-angle-side is an axiom for
   Bob and a known result for Dave. The angles along a line are an axiom
   for Bob and Dave and a known result for Carol. The area of a square is
   inside an axiom for Bob and a known result for Dave. None of them is
   wrong: it depends on where one starts. For a common ground each fact
   needs one status.
2. **The parallel axiom is said by one and carried by three.** Only Carol
   lists it. Bob has it inside his grid, and Dave inside two known results.
3. **What a length is.** Percy's town of square blocks has the same points
   and the same right angle, and the formula fails there, because length
   is counted along streets. So something on each list must say which
   length is meant. For Bob it is the grid with one unit on both axes,
   together with side-angle-side. For Dave it is that figures can be
   turned without change. For Carol it is that two equal angles make
   triangles similar. None of them says that this is what the item is for.

### Three findings before any proof

1. **There is no proof from topology alone.** Carol: bending turns the
   triangle 3, 4, 5 into 3, 4, 6, and topology cannot tell the two apart,
   so it cannot even state the theorem. Shearing fails the same way.
   Scaling is the first kind of change under which the theorem can be
   said. Her proof will be by similarity, and she will not call it
   topological.
2. **A proof by coordinates alone does not seem to exist at this level.**
   Bob: any rule that gives the length of a slanted segment from its
   coordinates is the theorem again. He brings in area and says his proof
   is less pure for it. He does not claim that no pure proof exists.
3. **The three proofs will stand on three different things:** Bob on a
   grid and area, Dave on area and motion, Carol on ratios alone, with no
   area at all.

## Act 2: three proofs

Opened 2026-11-23 03:26, on the user's word.

### What the user said after Act 1

The user is skeptical, and said so: much of this is prepared in advance,
and the difference between the proofs cannot be seen. Both are fair. All
of us are sessions of one model that has read every textbook proof of this
theorem, so that we agreed in Act 1 shows little. And two of the three
proofs may turn out to be one proof told twice.

So Act 2 has one aim above the others: **make the difference between the
proofs something that can be seen and checked,** not something a reader
has to take from the voices.

### Settled for this act

- Bob may use area. He says exactly which step needs it.
- Carol proves by similarity, and it is not called topological.
- For the common ground each fact has one status: side-angle-side, the
  angles along a straight line, the rules of area and the parallel axiom
  are axioms. That the angles of a triangle make two right angles is a
  known result, resting on the parallel axiom.

### To Bob, Dave and Carol

Prove the theorem, in your own way and voice, under `## Act 2` in your
file. Write without reading the others' Act 2. The form is fixed, so that
the three can be laid side by side.

1. **The idea in two sentences.** What your proof does, said to someone
   who will not read the steps.
2. **The steps, numbered.** Each step is one statement and its reason.
   The reason names the item of your own Act 1 list it rests on, as
   "item 9", or an earlier step, as "step 3". No step rests on a picture.
   A picture may stand beside the steps.
3. **Where the right angle is used.** Name the step. Say what happens at
   that step for an angle of 60° at C.
4. **Where flatness is used.** Name the step and the item.
5. **Used.** The items of your list that the steps cite, as a plain list of
   numbers.
6. **Not used.** The items of your list that no step cites. If your list
   was missing something the proof needed, add it here as a new numbered
   item and say that it is new.
7. **The one thing my proof needs that I expect the others can do
   without.** One sentence.

For Bob, one thing more. Having given up the distance formula and taken
area, your proof may come out as Dave's proof with coordinates laid over
it. Make the coordinates do real work: every "the pieces fit" and every
"this is a square" is to be shown by coordinates and inequalities, not by
a figure. At the end say honestly what in your proof is not a proof by
cutting and moving.

### To Percy

You do not read the proofs yet. Build the **common ground** in your file,
under `## Act 2`: one list of every fact on the three lists of Act 1,
each fact once, numbered G1, G2 and so on, each with the one status settled
above, and beside it the item numbers it has on Bob's, Dave's and Carol's
lists. Mark each fact that is false on a ball, and each fact that is false
in your town of square blocks. That list is what the proofs will be
measured against in Act 3.

When your part is written, send me one line: "Act 2 done".

## Act 2 closed: the proofs, side by side

Written 2026-11-23 03:31. Bob, Dave and Carol each wrote a proof without
reading the others. Percy merged the three lists of Act 1 into one common
ground of 37 facts, G1 to G37. I have read all four and translated each
proof's "Used" list into Percy's numbers. The translation is mine, and a
prover who finds an item placed wrongly should say so in Act 3.

### A correction to my closing of Act 1

I wrote that Bob's grid helps to fix which length is meant. Percy shows
that it does not: every fact about the grid is true in his town of square
blocks, because along a street the town's length is the difference of the
coordinates. What keeps the town out is side-angle-side, G19, for Bob and
Dave, and "two equal angles make similar triangles", G23, for Carol.
Percy is right and I was wrong.

### The three proofs

| | Bob | Dave | Carol |
|---|---|---|---|
| Idea | four triangles in a square of side a + b on a grid, the middle shown to be a square of side c by coordinates, the area counted twice | a square of side a + b cut in two ways, each with four copies of the triangle, so what is left is equal | the perpendicular from C cuts the triangle into two of its own shape, and equal ratios give a² and b² as c times a piece of c |
| Steps | 22 | 25 | 21 |
| Facts of the common ground used | 19 | 17 | 13 |
| New items the proof forced | 3 | 1 | 3 |
| Uses area | steps 18 to 21 only | throughout | never |
| Moves a figure | no | no | no |

### What each proof stands on

Of the 37 facts, 33 are used by at least one proof.

| Used by | Facts | How many |
|---|---|---|
| all three | G1 arithmetic, G6 right angle, G8 right triangle, G17 angles along a line, G20 the angles of a triangle | 5 |
| Bob and Dave, not Carol | G10 rectangle and square, G19 side-angle-side, G32 area exists, G34 areas of pieces add | 4 |
| Bob and Carol, not Dave | G4 length of a segment | 1 |
| Dave and Carol, not Bob | G16 lengths add along a line | 1 |
| Bob alone | G2 order, G11, G24, G25, G26, G27 the grid, G13 congruent by parts, G35 area of a rectangle, G37 area of a right triangle | 9 |
| Dave alone | G14 cutting, G28, G29, G30, G31 rectangles behave, G33 congruent figures have equal areas, G36 area of a square | 7 |
| Carol alone | G5 between, G7 triangle, G9 perpendicular, G15 similar, G22 one perpendicular, G23 two equal angles | 6 |

How much two proofs share, as the facts both use over the facts either
uses:

| Pair | Shared | Either | Share |
|---|---|---|---|
| Bob and Dave | 9 | 27 | 0.33 |
| Dave and Carol | 6 | 24 | 0.25 |
| Bob and Carol | 6 | 26 | 0.23 |

Bob and Dave draw the same figure, and both say so. They still share only
a third of their ground. They reach "the middle is a square" and "the
pieces fit" by different facts: Bob by the grid and inequalities, Dave by
the behaviour of rectangles and by straight cuts.

### Take a fact away: which proofs fall?

| Fact taken away | Bob | Dave | Carol |
|---|---|---|---|
| G20, the angles of a triangle | falls | falls | falls |
| G17, the angles along a line | falls | falls | falls |
| G19, side-angle-side | falls | falls | stands |
| G32 and G34, area | falls | falls | stands |
| G24, the grid | falls | stands | stands |
| G33, congruent figures have equal areas | stands | falls | stands |
| G23, two equal angles make similar triangles | stands | stands | falls |
| G22, one perpendicular from a point | stands | stands | falls |
| G16, lengths add along a line | stands | falls | falls |

This is the difference between the proofs, as a table. There are two
families: Bob and Dave fall together when area or side-angle-side is
taken, and Carol falls alone when similarity is taken.

### What came out that nobody planned

1. **All three lists of Act 1 were incomplete,** and all three provers
   had called theirs complete. Seven new items in all. Every one of them
   is about order or position: which of three points is between the
   others, when a point is inside an angle, that a straight cut of a
   convex figure makes exactly two pieces. These are the things a picture
   supplies without being asked. They showed only because each step had to
   name its item.
2. **Nobody moves anything.** Dave's axiom that a figure can be moved is
   not used: each triangle is shown to be a copy where it lies. Bob does
   not use that congruent figures have equal areas. The proof "by cutting
   and moving" has no moving in it.
3. **At 60° each proof fails at a named step, with a number.** For the
   triangle with all sides 1, Bob and Dave get a² + b² = 2 for a side that
   is no longer c, and Carol gets two cuts that overlap, c times 2 where c
   times 1 was needed.
4. **One fact is on nobody's list:** that a unit square has the area 1.
   Percy: the three rules of area stay true if every area is doubled, so
   Dave's "a square of side s has the area s²" cannot be proved from his
   own list.
5. **Three questions are left for Act 3.** Does Bob add lengths along a
   slanted line, which his list does not allow? Which fact puts a triangle
   where a prover wants it? And where does each proof say that length does
   not depend on direction?
6. **Bob's steps 7, 8, 10, 13, 14 and 16 are statements about polynomials**
   in a and b. A machine can check them. Bob tried them on random points
   and says that this is a check and not a proof.
